# Does the hair BCSDF attenuate light differently FORWARD (photon pass) than BACKWARD (mode R)?
#
# WHAT PROMPTED IT. At fur_creature's own adaptive gather radius, mode M reads exact on every
# unfurred surface (wall -0.05 %, floor +0.04 %) and 17-33 % TOO DARK on everything the light has
# to cross fur to reach. The deficit is ordered by how much fur is in the way: coat -17, skin -11,
# tan -23, belly -32, the belly lying under the densest coat. The footprint correction is provably
# inert there (coverage 1.0 at r = 0.0092; toggling the flag moves belly 0.2 points) and light-path
# bounce truncation is refuted (cap 32 vs 128 is byte-identical while cap 2 moves the image 20 %).
# What is left that fits an ordering in fur thickness is a per-strand transmission mismatch between
# the two directions of transport, which compounds with the number of strands crossed.
#
# THE DISCRIMINATOR IS STRAND COUNT, AND EVERY COUNT IS IN ONE FRAME. Four identical diffuse
# patches, each its own material NAME with a byte-identical BSDF, differing only in how many hair
# strands stand on them: 0, 10k, 40k, 160k per square metre (the creature's coats run ~175k/m^2).
# One mode-M render and one mode-R render score all four, so the photon count, the camera, the
# light and the exposure are literally the same numbers for every patch -- a confound cannot
# differ between arms that share a render.
#
# p0 IS THE BY-CONSTRUCTION NULL. It carries no fur at all, so mode M and mode R must agree on it
# to within noise for any reason whatsoever. If p0 moves, the rig is measuring something other
# than fur and no other column can be read. (fur_creature's wall and floor already pass this test
# at -0.05 % and +0.04 %, so the null is known to be achievable.)
#
# PREDICTION, REGISTERED BEFORE THE RUN:
#   M/R = 1.00 on p0, then falls monotonically with strand count -- p1 > p2 > p3 in deficit.
#   If instead all four patches show the SAME deficit, the cause is not per-strand and does not
#   compound, which refutes the transmission-mismatch story.
#   If p0 also moves, the rig is broken and nothing is concluded.
scene { units meters  spectral 360 830 1 }

# One BSDF, four names. Any difference between patches is strand count, never material.
material "p0" { type diffuse  reflect whitewall 0.75 }
material "p1" { type diffuse  reflect whitewall 0.75 }
material "p2" { type diffuse  reflect whitewall 0.75 }
material "p3" { type diffuse  reflect whitewall 0.75 }
material "hair" { type diffuse  reflect rgb 0.42 0.26 0.13 }

quad "q0" { origin 0.0 0 0   u 1 0 0   v 0 0 1   material p0 }
quad "q1" { origin 1.4 0 0   u 1 0 0   v 0 0 1   material p1 }
quad "q2" { origin 2.8 0 0   u 1 0 0   v 0 0 1   material p2 }
quad "q3" { origin 4.2 0 0   u 1 0 0   v 0 0 1   material p3 }

# Identical grooms; ONLY `count` differs. No droop/curl/clump, so the strands stand straight and
# the only thing scaling between patches is how many of them the light meets.
fur "f1" { on "q1"  material hair  count 10000   length 0.05  radius 0.0008  jitter 0.15  seed 1 }
fur "f2" { on "q2"  material hair  count 40000   length 0.05  radius 0.0008  jitter 0.15  seed 1 }
fur "f3" { on "q3"  material hair  count 160000  length 0.05  radius 0.0008  jitter 0.15  seed 1 }

# An ENV light, not a panel. The first draft used a broad area quad above the patches and the
# camera -- looking straight down -- saw nothing but the back of the light, which `-roiboxes`
# caught immediately by reporting 0 visible materials. An env light has no geometry to stand in
# the way and is the broadest source available, which is what a thin fiber needs: a small source
# leaves most of a strand's circumference in shadow and the coat reads black.
light env { spd preset:d65 }

camera "cam" {
    eye 2.6 7 0.5   look_at 2.6 0 0.5   up 0 0 1   fov_y 45
    mode M
    film { res 256 256 }
}
